29. Stresses that technological innovation, artificial intelligence and automation must always remain human-centred while safeguarding industrial competitiveness and that they require the continuous upskilling and reskilling of workers; calls for training policies that respond to actual labour market needs, prioritise employability and preserve the flexibility of national training systems; underlines that access to training should be promoted through cooperation between employers, workers and social partners, without imposing uniform EU obligations on enterprises;

---

16 Draghi, M., The future of European competitiveness, September 2024, p. 19
